const faq = [
  { q: 'What is the significance of the raven in Aboriginal culture?', a: 'The term "raven" in an Australian context typically refers to the Australian Raven (Corvus coronoides) — the largest Australian corvid. Aboriginal peoples across Australia have Dreaming stories involving corvids (ravens and crows), varying by region. In many traditions, Crow (Waa in Warlpiri and related languages) is a major Ancestor figure — a Trickster-transformer and culture hero, credited in some stories with introducing fire, death, or other fundamental aspects of the world. The crow/raven is one of the most mythologically significant birds in Australian Aboriginal tradition.' },
  { q: 'What is the Crow Dreaming story?', a: 'There are many Crow Dreaming stories across different language groups and regions — no single canonical version. In Warlpiri tradition (central Australia), Waa the Crow is a major ancestral being involved in creating aspects of the landscape and in conflict narratives with Eagle (Walawuru/Wedge-tailed Eagle). In southeastern Australian traditions, Crow is credited in some accounts with stealing fire and distributing it to humans. The specific stories vary substantially between language groups, as Aboriginal traditions are regionally distinct and not a single unified mythology.' },
  { q: 'Is the raven or crow more important in Aboriginal mythology?', a: 'The distinction between crow and raven species is typically not the focus of Aboriginal traditions, which relate to the bird as experienced — the large black corvid present in one\'s country. In areas where the Australian Raven (Corvus coronoides) is the dominant large corvid (south and southwest), the bird of the Dreaming stories is the raven. In areas where Australian Crow (Corvus orru) dominates (northern and inland), it is the crow. Scholars working in this area typically use "Crow" as the translation of the relevant Ancestor figure, regardless of exact species.' },
];
